rtw_write32_clr(rtwdev, REG_TXPAUSE, BIT_HIGH_QUEUE);
rtw_write32_set(rtwdev, REG_TXPAUSE, BIT_HIGH_QUEUE);
Reason:
These statements attempt to perform 32-bit read-modify-write operations on the register REG_TXPAUSE, whose address is not aligned to a 4-byte boundary. On architectures requiring strict alignment (e.g., ARM64), the PCI read/write functions (e.g., rtw_pci_read32) trigger an alignment fault when accessing such an unaligned address.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
rtw_write8_clr(rtwdev, REG_TXPAUSE, BIT_HIGH_QUEUE);
rtw_write8_set(rtwdev, REG_TXPAUSE, BIT_HIGH_QUEUE);
Reason:
Using 8-bit operations (rtw_write8_clr/rtw_write8_set) instead of 32-bit ones avoids the alignment problem because 8-bit accesses require only byte alignment, which the address of REG_TXPAUSE satisfies. This preserves the intended bit manipulation without causing hardware alignment faults.

